{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"chiselled-thrifty-gnat","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`tests/e2e/_toolchain.py::provision_toolchain` checks `_satisfied()` BEFORE taking the flock,\nand `_satisfied` treats the harness bundle as ready on mere existence\n(`(js_dir / BUNDLE_RELPATH).is_file()`). esbuild writes `dist/roundtrip.mjs` in place, so\nunder `-n 4 --dist worksteal` a worker can see the path exist while the lock holder is still\nwriting it, return early, and run `node dist/roundtrip.mjs` against a truncated file. The\nfailure would surface as a confusing harness/JSON error rather than as a provisioning problem.\n\nRaised as an LLM-Review advisory on change 2600 (`tests/e2e/_toolchain.py:151`). It did not\nblock that change and was deliberately left to land the priority-0 unblocker first; nothing\nabout it is fixed by what merged.\n\n## Approach\n\nDelete the unlocked fast path and evaluate `_satisfied()` INSIDE the lock. The fast path was\nan optimisation for the already-provisioned case, but an `fcntl.flock` on a local file costs\nmicroseconds and is taken a handful of times per run (once per xdist worker at collection,\nplus at most once per session in the fixture fallback), so the saving it buys does not pay for\nthe race it opens. A waiter blocks only while a peer is genuinely provisioning, and it waits\nduring COLLECTION, which is not on any test timeout budget — the property bug\n9a17-e0b3-7aa6-4091 established.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/e2e/_toolchain.py` — move the `_satisfied()` check inside `_install_lock`.\n- `tests/e2e/test_toolchain_provisioning.py` — extend the concurrency oracle.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe existing two-process concurrency test brackets only the install step. Extend the stub\n`npm` to bracket `run build` as well, and assert the whole event sequence across both\nprocesses is disjoint (no span of either step opens while another is open). Pre-fix, a second\nprocess returns as soon as the bundle path appears, so its span closes before the builder\nfinishes and the sequence interleaves; post-fix it cannot. Verify RED before GREEN.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `provision_toolchain` performs no readiness check outside the lock. Checked by the\n      extended two-process test, which must fail against the current code and pass after.\n- [ ] A caller never returns while a peer is mid-build. Checked by the disjoint-span assertion\n      over bracketed install AND build events from both processes.\n- [ ] No regression in the existing behaviour. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/e2e/test_toolchain_provisioning.py -q` and a cold\n      `pytest tests/e2e -n 4 --dist worksteal -q` completing with no worker kill.","id":"477b-4130-424d-41eb","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["bug","tests","concurrency"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"e2e toolchain provisioning has an unlocked fast path that can expose a half-written harness bundle"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788499963320750001,"uuid":"53c52249-9175-49e0-b799-47d1af1800b3"}